- How would you adapt this to support getMin() on a persistent stack?
- How would you test boundary cases (empty pop, getMin on empty)?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time analytics require fast percentile queries with inserts/deletes while handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression maps latencies to a compact domain.
- Fenwick tree maintains prefix counts efficiently.
- kth-order statistic via binary lifting yields the percentile value.
- Carefully handle erase when a value count reaches zero.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){ let idx=0; let bit=1; while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit<<=1; for(; bit>0; bit>>=1){ let nxt = idx + bit; if(nxt <= this.n && this.bit[nxt] < k){ idx = nxt; k -= this.bit[nxt]; } } return idx+1; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle unseen latency values without rebuilding the compression map?
- Compare this approach to a wavelet tree for large domains or streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mapping of high-level operations to a classic data-structure primitive (Fenwick tree) with dynamic growth.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree (Binary Indexed Tree)
- 1-based indexing
- dynamic growth and delta updates
- handling duplicates in values

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.ft=new Array(n+1).fill(0); this.arr=[] }
  append(v){ this.arr.push(v); const i=this.arr.length; this.add(i,v); }
  add(i,delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+=i&-i) this.ft[i]+=delta; }
  update(i,v){ const delta=v-this.arr[i-1]; this.arr[i-1]=v; this.add(i,delta); }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-=i&-i) s+=this.ft[i]; return s; }
  range(l,r){ return this.sum(r)-this.sum(l-1); }
}
```

